  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Strong westerly winds today could lead to some \nisolated power outages. Also, the strong wind coupled with the \ncolder temperatures will lead to dangerous wind chills in the \nmountains today.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nStrong westerly winds are currently increasing as a low-level\njet of strong winds aloft associated with a departing low moves\nover the area. The strongest winds are currently expected \naround mid-morning today before gradually tapering off this\nafternoon as the strong low pressure system exits the area. \nCurrently expecting gusts to between 40 and 50 mph over the \nhigher terrain across the north, with up to 65 mph possible at \nthe highest summits. A Wind Advisory is in effect for areas \nincluding and north of Millinocket and Greenville.\n\nOver interior Downeast and the Bangor region, expect westerly\nwind gusts between 30 and 40 mph but below wind advisory \ncriteria. It is likely that significant leaf loss will occur \nacross the region, especially where peak foliage has been \nreached. Gusty winds could also lead to some isolated power \noutages.\n\nWith the colder airmass moving in, the strong winds will lead \nto dangerously cold wind chills in the mountains through \ntonight. Katahdin will have wind chills in the teens even in \nthe middle of the day today. Hikers should be prepared for \nwinter conditions.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Frost expected Downeast tonight with low\ntemperatures approaching freezing.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure building in behind the departing low pressure\nsystem should lead to clear skies overnight which in combination\nwith decreasing winds should allow for strong overnight cooling.\nTemperatures are expected to dip into the low to mid 30s tonight\nover the interior Downeast where the growing season is still\nactive. Widespread frost is likely and thus a Frost Advisory \nhas been issued. For coastal Downeast, expect a more patchy \nfrost with temperatures in the mid to upper 30s, especially \nfurther inland. Did not have enough confidence yet to issue a \nFrost Advisory here due to some concerns about winds staying \nelevated which would counteract the overnight cooling.",
